## Upstream models

Operations Risk Monitor sends prompts to a language model chosen by whoever runs it. The model is
one line in config/settings.yaml under llm.model, routed through LiteLLM, and the matching key
comes from the operator's own environment. The lab does not train, fine-tune or distribute models,
and is not a provider of a general-purpose AI model under the EU AI Act. Obligations for the models
themselves sit with their providers, under their own terms:

- Anthropic (Claude) - https://www.anthropic.com/legal/commercial-terms
  Default configuration. Needs ANTHROPIC_API_KEY.
- OpenAI - https://openai.com/policies/terms-of-use
  Selected by setting llm.model to a gpt-* string. Needs OPENAI_API_KEY.
- Locally run open-weight models, for example via Ollama - under their own model licences
  Selected by setting llm.model to ollama/<name> and LLM_API_BASE to the local endpoint. No key
  leaves the machine. Set include_model: false in config/disclosure.yaml if you would rather the
  local model name did not travel in output metadata.

Any other provider LiteLLM speaks will work the same way, and the same split applies: the model is
its provider's responsibility, the marking of what comes back is ours.

Output produced with these models is marked per EU AI Act Article 50. See COMPLIANCE.md.

## Bundled third-party material

None. Runtime dependencies are declared in requirements.txt and install from PyPI under their own
licences. The brand logo at input/logo@2x.png, when present, is supplied by the operator.
